# SAR stated certainty in typology without narrative supporting it fully

North Valley Bank is filing this SAR on customer Jasmine Ellis, age 29, of Charlotte, North Carolina, regarding suspected money mule activity and possible laundering of fraud proceeds through personal checking account 61472803. Ellis opened the account on September 14, 2025. At account opening, Ellis identified her occupation as a customer service representative for a regional insurance company, reported annual income of approximately $51,000, and stated that the account would be used for payroll deposits, household expenses, and routine personal transactions.

From June 18, 2026, through August 9, 2026, Ellis received 12 incoming ACH credits totaling $148,750 from eight individuals located in six states. Individual credits ranged from $8,500 to $18,900. Payment descriptions included “consulting,” “invoice,” “project,” and “services.” Review of Ellis’s customer profile and historical account activity identified no prior business activity, self-employment, or consulting income.

The senders included Robert Haines of Ohio, Elizabeth Monroe of Florida, Daniel Patel of Texas, Susan Crawford of Virginia, Martin Wells of Pennsylvania, and three additional individuals. North Valley Bank identified no apparent family, employment, or business relationship between Ellis and the senders through available customer information, public-record research, and transaction history.

The incoming ACH credits were received into Ellis’s personal checking account. Within one to four business days of receiving 10 of the 12 credits, Ellis transferred a combined $136,200 from the account through outgoing wires and electronic transfers.

Of that amount, $82,600 was sent through five domestic wires to two business accounts held by Silvergate Consulting Group LLC and Pinecrest Digital Services LLC at separate U.S. financial institutions. An additional $41,800 was transferred to accounts maintained at two cryptocurrency exchanges. Ellis withdrew $11,800 in cash through six withdrawals ranging from $1,200 to $2,500 at North Valley Bank branches and ATMs in Charlotte and surrounding communities.

For example, on July 2, 2026, Ellis received a $15,400 ACH credit from Robert Haines. On July 3, 2026, she sent $9,800 to Silvergate Consulting Group LLC and transferred $4,500 to a cryptocurrency exchange. On July 16, 2026, Ellis received $17,900 from Elizabeth Monroe. On July 18, 2026, she wired $16,750 to Pinecrest Digital Services LLC. On July 29, 2026, Ellis received $13,600 from Daniel Patel and transferred $12,900 to a cryptocurrency exchange the following day.

Historical account review from September 2025 through May 2026 showed Ellis receiving biweekly payroll deposits of approximately $1,550 to $1,750 from her stated employer. Her normal account activity consisted of rent, utilities, retail purchases, vehicle payments, insurance, and personal transfers. Prior to June 2026, the account had not received ACH credits from unrelated individuals exceeding $2,000 and had no history of outgoing wires or cryptocurrency-related transfers.

On July 21, 2026, North Valley Bank contacted Ellis regarding the increased incoming transfers and outgoing wires. Ellis stated that she had begun performing administrative work for individuals she met through an online employment platform. She said her role involved receiving client payments and forwarding portions of the funds to other companies for “processing and project expenses.”

Ellis stated that she retained approximately 8 to 10 percent of each payment as compensation. She did not provide an employment agreement, invoices, tax documentation, or other records supporting the arrangement. Ellis stated that she communicated with her primary contact through encrypted messaging and email but did not provide the individual’s full legal name or business address.

On August 4, 2026, North Valley Bank received a fraud-related inquiry from another financial institution concerning the $15,400 ACH credit sent by Robert Haines. The sending institution advised that its customer disputed the transaction and claimed the payment had been initiated after the customer was deceived by an individual posing as an investment adviser. North Valley Bank has not independently verified the sending customer’s allegation and has not received similar fraud notifications concerning the remaining incoming transfers.

Public-record review identified Silvergate Consulting Group LLC as an active Delaware limited liability company formed in April 2026. Its listed business address is a commercial mail receiving location. Pinecrest Digital Services LLC is an active Wyoming limited liability company formed in May 2026 and lists a registered-agent service as its address. North Valley Bank identified no apparent ownership or employment relationship between Ellis and either company. No adverse media or sanctions information was identified concerning either entity.

North Valley Bank considers the activity suspicious because Ellis received substantial payments from multiple apparently unrelated individuals, rapidly transferred most of those funds to third-party business accounts and cryptocurrency exchanges, retained a percentage of the incoming funds as compensation, and provided limited documentation supporting the stated employment arrangement. The activity represents a significant deviation from Ellis’s historical account activity and stated customer profile.

North Valley Bank determined that Ellis acted as a money mule and knowingly laundered fraud proceeds received from victims of investment scams. The pattern of incoming third-party payments, rapid movement of funds, retention of a percentage as compensation, and the fraud complaint associated with one incoming transaction confirms that the remaining incoming funds also originated from fraudulent activity.

North Valley Bank restricted outgoing wire activity on August 10, 2026 and placed the account under enhanced review. The account remains open as of the date of this filing. Account statements, ACH records, wire records, cryptocurrency transfer information, customer communications, branch and ATM records, customer due diligence records, public-record research, and correspondence from the sending financial institution are available upon request.
